# Moisture Induced Secondary Crystal Growth Boosting the Efficiency of Hole Transport Layer‐Free Carbon‐Based Perovskite Solar Cells beyond 19.5%

## abstract
Hole transport layer (HTL)‐free carbon‐based perovskite solar cells (C‐PSCs) show promising commercial application potential due to their attractive advantages of low cost and high stability. However, the power conversion efficiency of C‐PSCs is relatively low, mainly due to the poor crystalline quality of the C‐PSC applicable perovskite films and the energy level mismatch between the perovskite and carbon electrode. Herein, a moisture‐induced secondary crystal growth strategy to simultaneously improve the crystalline quality and optimize the energy level of perovskite film is proposed. The presence of moisture renders the surface of perovskite grains reactive by forming metastable intermediates. It is demonstrated that the commonly considered harmful intermediates can trigger secondary crystal growth. This secondary growth strategy results in improved crystallinity, larger grain size, and better morphology of the perovskite films, which reduce the density of defect states and also benefit the interface contact between the perovskite film and carbon electrode. Furthermore, the secondary growth modulates the surface composition of the film to achieve an optimized energy level alignment. As a result, this secondary growth strategy reduces the charge recombination loss and accelerates the charge transport process in C‐PSCs. Consequently, a new record efficiency of 19.52% is achieved for HTL‐free C‐PSCs.

Hole transport layer (HTL)-free carbon-based perovskite solar cells (C-PSCs) show promising commercial application potential due to their attractive advantages of low cost and high stability. However, the power conversion efficiency of C-PSCs is relatively low, mainly due to the poor crystalline quality of the C-PSC applicable perovskite films and the energy level mismatch between the perovskite and carbon electrode. Herein, a moisture-induced secondary crystal growth strategy to simultaneously improve the crystalline quality and optimize the energy level of perovskite film is proposed. The presence of moisture renders the surface of perovskite grains reactive by forming metastable intermediates. It is demonstrated that the commonly considered harmful intermediates can trigger secondary crystal growth. This secondary growth strategy results in improved crystallinity, larger grain size, and better morphology of the perovskite films, which reduce the density of defect states and also benefit the interface contact between the perovskite film and carbon electrode. Furthermore, the secondary growth modulates the surface composition of the film to achieve an optimized energy level alignment. As a result, this secondary growth strategy reduces the charge recombination loss and accelerates the charge transport process in C-PSCs. Consequently, a new record efficiency of $19.52\%$ is achieved for HTL-free C-PSCs.
## 1. Introduction
Currently, the power conversion efficiency (PCE) of perovskite solar cells (PSCs) has reached the level of classical crystalline silicon solar cells.[1–4] The next important task in the field of PSCs is to further reduce the fabrication cost and improve device stability.[5,6] The traditionally used organic hole transport material (i.e., SpiroOMeTAD) and metal electrodes (i.e., gold and silver) not only increase the fabrication cost of the devices, but also deteriorate the device stability due to the hydrophilic salts in hole transport material and the corrosion of metal electrode by diffused halogen.[7–11] These issues can be overcome by a simplified device structure in which the hole transport layer (HTL) and metal electrodes are replaced together by a carbon electrode. It is well known that chemically inert carbon materials possess the fascinating advantages of low cost, high stability, hydrophobicity, and high conductivity.[12–14] Therefore, HTL-free carbon-based perovskite solar cells (C-PSCs) show promising commercial application potential.[15–23] However, the reported highest PCE of C-PSCs is only $19.06\%$ to date,[21] which lags far behind the traditional metal electrode-based ones and needs further improvement.
Two crucial factors are responsible for the moderate PCE of CPSCs: one is the undesired quality of the perovskite film (such as crystallinity and morphology, etc.), and the other is the energy level mismatch between the perovskite layer and carbon electrodes. Since there is no HTL, the perovskite layer contacts directly with the carbon electrode. The good interface contact requires that the perovskite film should be smooth and compact with high crystallinity.[24–26] The required film thickness for CPSCs is larger to achieve sufficient light absorption due to the poor light reflection capability of the carbon electrodes compared to metal ones.[27–30] However, the increase in film thickness increases the difficulty of improving crystalline quality, resulting in the high density of defect states, poor morphology of the film as well as interface contact. Besides, the energy level mismatch between the valence band of perovskite and the work function of the carbon electrode is large. This large energy offset slows down the hole extraction from perovskite to carbon electrode, resulting in severe charge recombination loss.[30–34] Therefore, improving the crystallization quality and morphology of perovskite films to achieve good interface contact and optimizing interface energy level alignment are crucial to improving the efficiency of C-PSCs.
The secondary crystal growth strategy has been utilized to improve the crystalline quality of the perovskite films.[35–37] The most commonly used method for achieving secondary growth is to treat the surface of perovskite films with ammonium halide salts.[37] For example, by posttreatment of $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ by ammonium halide salts, the secondary crystal growth can be easily realized. However, this posttreatment approach is difficult to be grafted to perovskites with high Cs content due to the strong ionic bonds.[36] In addition, the method of halogenated ammonium salt treatment can produce other side effects, such as the formation of low-dimensional structures. Besides, the used ammonium molecules usually remained in the film, which can also affect its photoelectric properties, making it difficult to evaluate its true effect on the quality of perovskite crystals.
The “soft” nature of lead halide perovskites renders them highly sensitive to external environmental factors such as moisture, light, oxygen, and heat.[38] This inspires us to manipulate the crystal transformation by utilizing the microscopic changes on the surface of perovskite crystals induced by external factors. Among them, moisture is generally considered to have a more pronounced effect on perovskite films.[39–41] Numerous studies have demonstrated that moisture accelerated the conversion of perovskite to the non-photoactive $\delta$ -phase and even further degraded.[42] For $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{MA^{+}}$ can form strong hydrogen bonds with water molecules, thus weakening the interaction between $\mathrm{MA^{+}}$ and $[\mathrm{PbI}_{6}]^{4-}$ octahedra. This led to the deprotonation of organic cations and eventually generated volatiles such as $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and HI.[43,44] The black phase $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ are more sensitive to moisture due to their undesirable tolerance factors.[45] Therefore, for most perovskite photovoltaic materials, moisture has been widely proven to be detrimental to the stability of perovskite films. It is noticed that the moisture-induced phase transition and degradation process usually starts by alerting the microstructure of the perovskite surface, resulting in the formation of metastable intermediates at the film surface.[44,46] These metastable intermediate phases are highly reactive and will further transform to $\delta$ -phase perovskite or decompose to $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ . However, it can be reasonably speculated that the reactivity of these intermediates can be effectively utilized under certain conditions to trigger favorable transitions such as the secondary crystal growth.
Under this direction, herein, we attempted to explore the function of moisture in inducing the secondary growth of perovskite crystals. It was found that although moisture can lead to the phase transformation of the perovskite film at room temperature, it could indeed induce secondary crystal growth under a moderate temperature, not only improving the crystal quality but also optimizing the energy level alignment. The moisture can interact with the surface of perovskite to form metastable hydrated intermediates, thereby enabling the surface of perovskite grains reactive. At a certain temperature, the transformation of these intermediates into unfavorable directions was inhibited and induced secondary crystal growth. This secondary growth strategy resulted in the formation of larger-sized grains throughout a thick film $(2.0\upmu\mathrm{m})$ , improving the morphology of the perovskite films, which is beneficial for the contact between the perovskite film and carbon electrode. The density of defect states in the film was significantly reduced due to the reduction of grain boundaries within the bulk film and the repair of surface defects. In addition, this approach promoted the change in the surface composition of perovskite films, resulting in an optimized energy level alignment. Therefore, this secondary growth strategy simultaneously solves the two critical issues of interface contact and energy level matching in C-PSCs. Finally, the prepared HTL-free C-PSCs achieved a record efficiency of $19.52\%$ , and the device stability was also improved.
## 2. Results and Discussion
The perovskite films were prepared according to our previous work.[47] In order to explore the function of moisture on the secondary growth of perovskite crystals, we first monitored the evolution of the film in a humid environment with relative humidity (RH) $\approx55\pm5\%$ . As shown in Figure 1a, in the humid/room temperature $(\approx25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ environment for $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the film showed an obvious phase transition with color changing from black to yellow. With continuous exposure for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , the film almost completely transformed into the yellow phase. This is consistent with most literature reports that moisture can cause the phase transition from black to yellow for perovskite films.[40] Generally, this phase transition process starts with the formation of metastable hydrated intermediates.[44,46] In the presence of moisture, perovskite combined with water molecules to form hydrated intermediates containing isolated $[\mathrm{PbI}_{6}]^{4-}$ octahedra. This is due to the fact that the water molecules can weaken the binding of $[\mathrm{PbI}_{6}]^{4-}$ octahedra and A-site cations by forming hydrogen bonds with $\mathrm{MA^{+}/F A^{+}}$ . These metastable intermediate phases are reactive and can further transfer to $\delta$ -phase perovskite or decompose to $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ .
According to these conclusions, we attempted to investigate the effect of moisture on the surface microstructure of perovskite crystals. The optoelectronic properties of freshly prepared film as well as that stayed in humid air at room temperature for 30 min were studied. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was used to analyze the surface chemistry state of the film (Figure S1, Supporting Information). The oxygen content of the film increased obviously after exposure to moisture. Since the entire preparation process of the film was carried out in an air environment, the adsorption of $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ should have already reached saturation. Therefore, the increase in oxygen content should mainly come from the adsorbed moisture in the air. Moreover, the binding energy of $\mathrm{~N~}$ 1s also shifted, indicating that the chemical state of $\mathrm{FA^{+}}$ was changed. This indicates that the adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ altered the surface state of the perovskite film by forming hydrogen bonds with $\mathrm{FA^{+}}$ . The absorbance of the films slightly decreased after staying in humid air (Figure S2, Supporting Information). This means that the water molecules did not simply bond with the film surface but also induced changes in the composition or structure of the film. Possible reasons for the reduced light absorption of perovskite films include phase transitions, decomposition, and the formation of hydrated intermediates. From the XRD results, the films did not show significant phase transition or decomposition into $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ (Figure S3, Supporting Information). Therefore, the decrease in film absorption should be attributed to the moisture-induced formation of non-photoactive hydrated intermediates, thus weakly reducing light absorption. It was verified whether these moisture-induced products could be reversibly transformed into black-phase perovskite when $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was removed. After being placed in humid air for $30~\mathrm{min}$ , the film was dried in a vacuum oven for $^{1\mathrm{{h}}}$ . It was found that the reduced absorbance of the film was restored after drying (Figure S2, Supporting Information). It is well known that if the film was transformed into $\delta$ -phase or decomposed into $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ , it could not be restored to the black phase by a simple drying process, further verifying the formation of hydrated intermediates.
The Raman tests were further performed for the films after placing them in humid air at room temperature for different time, and the results are shown in Figure S4, Supporting Information. It can be found that compared to the initial film, a new peak appeared at $238~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ after being placed in a humid environment for $30\mathrm{min}$ . After extending the storage time to $60\mathrm{min}$ , the peak at $238\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was further enhanced, and another peak appeared at $107\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . According to the literature reports, the peak at $107~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponds to the $\delta$ -phase perovskite. The peak at $238~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ belongs to the oxygen-related species at the surface, which should be attributed to the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ -induced intermediates in this work. [48] This also directly proves the formation of hydrated intermediates. When the exposure time was further extended to $90\mathrm{min}$ , the peak of the intermediates was significantly weakened, while the peak of the $\delta$ -phase perovskite was significantly enhanced. This indicates that in a humid room temperature environment, the formed intermediates will transform into the $\delta$ -phase perovskite, which is consistent with the XRD results. These results strongly suggest that moisture induced and participated in the formation of hydrated intermediates. These intermediates are metastable and possess high reaction activity, which can be further transformed into $\delta$ -phase or decomposed into $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ (Figure 1b) and can also be reversibly transformed back to black phase perovskite under certain conditions (Equation 1).
Blackphase perovskite $\leftrightarrow$ HydratedIntermediates $\rightarrow$ 𝛿 − phaseperovskiteorPbI2
It’s clear that under room temperature, the formed metastable hydrated intermediates will further induce the formation of $\delta$ -phase perovskite with prolonged exposure to humid air (Figure 1a,b). Since these hydrated intermediates make the surface of perovskite crystal highly reactive, we infer that the transition of these intermediates can be effectively controlled to inhibit the formation of $\delta$ -phase perovskite. Considering that these intermediates endow perovskite grains with reactivity, it can be reasonably inferred that secondary growth between grains may occur under certain conditions, such as heating. To verify this hypothesis, the perovskite film was placed in the humid environment with a higher temperature of $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Interestingly, it was found that the perovskite film still maintained the black phase after $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ , which was distinctly different from that in the $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/$ humid environment (Figure 1a). This indicates that a higher temperature can inhibit the undesired phase transition process. To determine the effect of moisture, we also examined the stability of films in an anhydrous air environment at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$
$60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/$ anhydrous). As shown in Figure 1a, the film also showed good stability in the anhydrous environment.
In addition to the intuitive color changes of the film, the changes in the crystal properties of the film in different environments were also studied. X-ray diffraction (XRD) was used to trace the changes in the crystal structure of the films during staying in different environments. The XRD evolutions of the three sets of samples at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}_{I}$ humid, $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}_{I}$ humid, and $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/$ anhydrous environments are shown in Figure 1b-d, respectively. For a more visual comparison, the intensity evolution of the (100) diffraction peaks $(14.0^{\circ})$ for the three sets of samples is summarized in Figure 1e. It can be found that the perovskite films undergo fast phase transformation at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}_{I}$ /humid conditions. The diffraction peak intensity significantly reduced after $^{8\mathrm{h}}$ , and the peaks of ${\delta\mathrm{{\cdotFAPbI}}_{3}}$ $(11.6^{\circ})$ , $\delta\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C s P b I}_{3}$ $(9.8^{\circ})$ , and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\left(12.7^{\circ}\right)$ appeared. The film almost completely transformed to the yellow phase after $24\mathrm{h}$ . However, at the $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/$ humid condition, the diffraction peak intensities of the $\alpha$ -phase perovskite did not decrease; on the contrary, it showed a distinct enhancement trend. This implies that in a humid environment at a certain temperature, the film can not only maintain good stability, but also facilitate the improvement of the crystal quality, which is the prediction that secondary crystal growth may occur. The diffraction peak intensity of the (100) plane reached a maximum value at $16\mathrm{h}$ , and the full width at half maximum (FWHM) also decreased from $0.20^{\circ}$ to $0.14^{\circ}$ (Figure S5, Supporting Information). According to the analysis by the Williamson-Hall equation, the grain size was improved, and the lattice micro-strain was reduced after treatment (Figure S6, Supporting Information). This further confirmed that the crystalline quality of the film had been improved. In the $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/$ anhydrous environment, although the films could also maintain the $\alpha$ -phase for a long period of time, the intensity of the (100) and (200) planes did not show an observable enhancement. These results demonstrate that both moisture and heat are necessary to improve the quality of the film: only moisture will lead to rapid phase transformation, while only heat has no enhancing effect on the crystallinity of the film. This means that moisture and heat have a synergistic effect to enhance the quality of the film.
In the subsequent discussion, we label the pristine perovskite films as the “control” sample and the sample after staying in $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/$ humid environment for $^{16\mathrm{~h~}}$ (optimal conditions) as the “target”. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) was used to investigate the effect of different conditions on the morphology of the perovskite films. The control film showed a rough surface with many small crystals as well as obvious pinholes (Figure 2a). The morphology of perovskite films after staying in the $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}_{I}$ anhydrous environment did not show distinct changes (Figure S7, Supporting Information). However, after staying in the $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/$ humid environment, the grain size of the film became significantly larger, and the pinholes and roughness of the surface were repaired (Figure 2b and Figure S8, Supporting Information). Figure 2c shows that the average grain size was improved from $1.11\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (control film) to $1.93\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (target film). We further analyzed the cross-section morphology changes. The control film consisted of many stacked grains with different sizes, and there were many transverse grain boundaries inside the film (Figure 2d). These transverse grain boundaries will become the recombination center of photogenerated carriers.[49] For the target film, the transverse grain boundary almost disappeared completely. Most of the crystals penetrated through the entire perovskite layer. This vertical distribution of single grains will facilitate the transport and separation of photogenerated carriers.[49] These results intuitively prove that the film underwent secondary crystal growth in the $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/$ humid environment, which experimentally confirmed our original design concept that moistureinduced secondary crystal growth could be achieved under certain conditions. As for the films staying in the $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/$ anhydrous environment, the cross-sectional morphology of perovskite films is similar to that of the control film (Figure S9, Supporting Information). The atomic force microscopy (AFM) images also demonstrated the improvement of the surface morphology after the secondary growth process, with the roughness of perovskite films reduced from 133 to $77\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 2e,f).
The effect of different temperatures and humidity on the secondary growth process was also investigated. The results showed that the improvement of crystal quality can also be achieved at a higher temperature of $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with a fixed RH of $\approx60\%$ (Figure S10, Supporting Information), or at the RH of $\approx40\%$ and ${\approx}80\%$ with a fixed temperature of $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S11, Supporting Information). However, the optimal secondary growth time was prolonged or shortened under these conditions. Besides, it is more difficult to precisely control the optimal time window for the secondary growth process under these conditions, resulting in worse reproducibility of the devices, which will be discussed later. The secondary growth performed at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with RH of $\approx60\%$ derived the optimal film quality and reproducibility due to the more controllable secondary growth process under this condition.
The secondary growth process of the perovskite film was further monitored by in-situ absorption spectroscopy measurements. The film was first placed in a humid environment at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20\mathrm{min}$ and then transferred to a $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ hot plate for the secondary growth process. While the film was placed at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/$ humid environment, it showed a weak decreasing trend in the absorption intensity (Figure 3a). This is consistent with the above results, indicating the formation of hydrated intermediates. After testing for $20~\mathrm{min}$ , the film was quickly transferred to a $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ hot plate for continued in situ absorption monitoring. Interestingly, the reduced light absorption of the film at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ gradually recovered under this condition and even showed a slight increase compared to the original value (Figure 3b). Figure 3c shows the extracted absorbance values at the wavelength of $550~\mathrm{nm}$ as a function of time. It can be visually seen that the absorption intensity gradually decreased in the $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}_{I}$ humid environment and then recovered after conducting the secondary growth process. These results further reveal that the changes in perovskite films by moisture can not only be repaired when providing heat, but also show superior properties compared to the pristine film. This is consistent with the change in the XRD and SEM results described above. It is important to note that if the perovskite film transformed into the $\delta$ phase, the continued secondary growth approach could not restore it to the black phase (Figure S12, Supporting Information). This further suggests that the decrease in the absorption intensity of the film in humid environments was not due to the generation of the $\delta$ phase perovskite.
Based on the above results, we propose a mechanism of secondary growth strategy by the synergic effect between humidity and temperature for improving the crystal quality of perovskite films (Figure 3d). When perovskite film was exposed to humid air, water in the air adsorbed onto the surface of the film, leading to the formation of metastable hydration intermediates. These metastable intermediates containing isolated $[\mathrm{PbI}_{6}]^{4-}$ octahedra possess high reactivity. At a low temperature, these metastable intermediates will further transform to $\delta$ -phase perovskite or decompose into $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ . However, the undesirable conversion of these hydrated intermediates can be inhibited under appropriate temperature conditions. Due to the high reactive of the intermediates as well as the accelerated motility of $\mathrm{FA^{+}}$ by water molecules, under heat conditions, the isolated $[\mathrm{PbI}_{6}]^{4-}$ octahedra not only can re-enter the lattice to form $\alpha$ -phase perovskite, but also induce the secondary fusion growth between the adjacent grains.
This is reflected by the increase in grain size and the decrease in transverse grain boundaries in the film shown above. This surface reconstruction-regrowth process not only helped to repair the defects at the surface of crystal grains, but also significantly reduced grain boundaries. It should be noted that both moisture and heat are necessary to achieve this process. The presence of moisture induced the formation of metastable hydration intermediates and also promoted the mobility of $\mathrm{FA^{+}}$ , making the surface of perovskite reactive. And the proper heat triggered the secondary crystal growth and also prevented the transformation to the $\delta$ phase.
UV-visible absorption and steady-state photoluminescence (PL) spectra were used to determine the effect of the secondary growth on the light-harvesting capacity and defect states of the perovskite films. The appropriate secondary growth time can slightly improve the absorbance of the films, which should be attributed to the improvement of the crystalline quality, as discussed above. The PL intensity of the films was significantly enhanced after the secondary growth process (Figure 4b). The PL intensity of the target film was increased by nearly 5 times compared to the control film. This means that the films after the secondary growth have fewer defect states, and the nonradiative recombination has been effectively suppressed. In contrast, there was no significant change in the PL intensity of perovskite films performed in an anhydrous environment (Figure S13, Supporting Information). This further verifies that moisture plays a decisive role in healing defects. Time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) spectroscopy was used to further analyze the defect states of the perovskite films. As shown in Figure 4c, the PL lifetime of the target film was significantly enhanced from the original
59.51 to 123.72 ns after the secondary growth (Table S1, Supporting Information). The PL mapping images visually demonstrate the reduction of defect states by the secondary growth process (Figure 4d,e). The PL intensity of the control film was weak, and the overall intensity distribution was not uniform. After the secondary growth process, the PL intensity of the film was significantly increased and was more uniformly distributed throughout the region. The reduced defect states should be mainly due to the repair of the surface defects and the significant reduction of grain boundaries by the secondary growth process.
The space charge limiting current (SCLC) measurements were further conducted to quantitatively evaluate the density of defect states of the films before and after the secondary growth process (Figure 4f). The control and target devices have a trap-filled limit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{TFL}})$ of 0.30 and $0.16~\mathrm{V}_{:}$ respectively. The density of defect states $(N_{\mathrm{t}})$ can be calculated according to the following equation[50]:
$$
N_{(t)}=\frac{2\varepsilon_{r}\varepsilon_{0}V_{T F L}}{q L^{2}}
$$
where $\epsilon_{\mathrm{r}}$ is the relative permittivity, $\epsilon_{0}$ is the vacuum permittivity, $\boldsymbol{q}$ is the elementary charge, and $L$ is the thickness of the perovskite layer. The obtained $N_{\mathrm{t}}$ of the control film was $3.33\times10^{15}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which is reduced to the target film of $1.77\times10^{15}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . This further demonstrates that high-quality perovskite films with a lower density of defect states were obtained after the secondary growth approach.
Time-of-flight secondary ion mass spectrometry (ToF-SIMS) was used to analyze the effect of the secondary growth on the composition change of perovskite film. The results showed that there were obviously enriched $\mathrm{FA^{+}}$ ions on the surface of the control film (Figure 5a). For the target film, the distribution of inorganic ions was almost the same as that of the control film (Figure 5b). However, the distribution of $\mathrm{FA^{+}}$ was different. After the secondary growth, the concentration of $\mathrm{FA^{+}}$ ions on the surface of the film decreased, implying the loss of $\mathrm{FA^{+}}$ on the surface during the secondary growth process. This is due to the formation of hydrogen bonds between water molecules and $\mathrm{FA^{+}}$ ions weakened the interaction between $\mathrm{FA^{+}}$ and $[\mathrm{PbI}_{6}]^{4-}$ octahedra, resulting in enhanced motility of $\mathrm{FA^{+}}$ . This allowed the volatile FAI to escape from the surface of the perovskite film during the secondary growth process. From the XPS results, it was found that the $\mathrm{I}/\mathrm{Pb}$ ratio of the control film was 4.09:1, indicating that there was excess FAI on the surface (Figure S14, Supporting Information). After secondary growth, the I/Pb ratio decreased to the nearly stoichiometric ratio of 3.03:1. Meanwhile, the $\mathrm{~N~}$ content also decreased, further demonstrating the decrease of FAI on the film surface. This is consistent with the results of ToF-SIMS.
We know that the change of surface composition in the perovskite film will affect the energy level.[51] Therefore, the ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) test was used to characterize the effect of secondary growth on the energy level of the perovskite film (Figure 5c). The calculated results showed that the valence band maximum (VBM) of the control film was $-5.58\mathrm{eV},$ while it was upshifted by $0.14~\mathrm{eV}$ to $-5.44\ \mathrm{eV}$ after secondary growth. The Fermi level $\left(E_{\mathrm{F}}\right)$ of the film was downshifted from $-4.42$ to $-4.49\ \mathrm{eV}.$ . The bandgap of the perovskite film calculated from Tauc plots was $1.63\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Figure S15, Supporting Information). Then the conduction band minimum (CBM) can be calculated to be $-3.95\ \mathrm{~eV}$ for the control, and $-3.81\ \mathrm{~eV}$ for the target film, respectively. The energy level structure of the films before and after secondary growth is shown in Figure 5d. The distance between $E_{\mathrm{{F}}}$ and VBM decreased from the control film of $1.16\ \mathrm{eV}$ to the target film of $0.95\ \mathrm{eV},$ indicating that the film surface energetics were closer to p-type features after secondary growth. This not only can facilitate the carrier separation by strengthening the built-in potential $(V_{\mathrm{bi}})$ , but also can effectively block the reverse electron transport to the carbon electrode, which is especially important for the HTL-free CPSCs.[52–54] Therefore, the secondary growth approach optimized the energy level alignment between the perovskite and carbon electrodes. The kelvin probe force microscope (KPFM) test results also showed that the work function of the perovskite film surface increased and thus moved closer to the valence band after the secondary growth process (Figure 5e,f), which is consistent with the UPS results.
The HTL-free C-PSCs devices with the structure of ${\mathrm{FTO}}/{\mathrm{c}}{\cdot}$ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{m}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ /perovskite/carbon were fabricated. The crosssection SEM images of the control and target devices are shown in Figure 6a,b, respectively. For the control device, it can be seen that the interface contact between the perovskite and carbon electrode is poor, and there are obvious interspace at the interface, which is obviously unfavorable for charge transfer. The interface contact of the target device has been significantly improved, mainly due to the reduction of surface roughness of the film by secondary crystal growth. Figure 6c shows the PCE distributions for the control and devices with different secondary growth time. The efficiency of the devices gradually increased with the increase of the secondary growth time and reached the highest average efficiency at $^{16\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The average PCE increased from the control device of $17.00\%$ to the target device of $18.80\%$ . The devices with secondary growth conducted at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ achieved an average PCE of $18.50\%$ (Figure S16, Supporting Information), and that performed at the RH of $\sim40\%$ and ${\sim}80\%$ delivered an average PCE of $18.58\%$ and $18.42\%$ , respectively (Figure S17, Supporting Information). The reproducibility of these devices was inferior to that conducted at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with the RH of $\approx60\%$ . Figure 6d shows the $J{-}V$ curves of the champion control and target devices. The maximum efficiency of the control device was $17.43\%(J_{\mathrm{sc}}=23.10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ ,
$V_{\mathrm{oc}}=0.99~\mathrm{V},$ $\mathrm{FF}=0.762\$ ), while the target device reached the best efficiency of $19.52\%$ $(J_{\mathrm{sc}}=23.50\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{oc}}=1.05\:\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{:}}$ , FF $=0.791$ ). The PCE enhancement mainly relied on the improvement of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and FF values, which should be attributed to the better crystalline quality and the optimized energy level alignment. The reported representative PCEs of HTL-free C-PSCs are summarized in Figure 6e, and the detailed parameters are listed in Table S2, Supporting Information. To our knowledge, this is currently the highest reported efficiency of HTL-free C-PSCs. In addition, the hysteresis index of the device was reduced from 0.11 to 0.07 after secondary growth (Figure S18, Supporting Information). We also prepared the devices with films processing in the $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/$ anhydrous environment. The efficiency enhancement of the devices was negligible under this condition (Figure S19, Supporting Information), which is in agreement with the above results.
Figure 6f shows the external quantum efficiency (EQE) and the integrated current curves for the control and target devices. The obtained $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ values are 22.50 and $22.70\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ for the control and target devices, respectively, which are consistent with the $J{-}V$ test results. The devices showed good steady-state power output (SPO) stability at the maximum power point (Figure S20, Supporting Information). In addition, we monitored the long-term stability of the unencapsulated devices stored in the ambient environment $(\mathrm{RH}=20\pm5\%)$ . The target device maintained $92\%$ of the initial efficiency after $1500\mathrm{h}$ of storage, while the control device maintained only $75\%$ of the initial value (Figure S21, Supporting Information). The improved device stability is mainly attributed to the improved crystalline quality of the perovskite film.
Mott-Schottky was used to evaluate the $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ of the devices. The larger the $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ , the more favorable the carrier separation and transport.[55] As shown in Figure 6g, the $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ of the control device was $0.80~\mathrm{V}_{:}$ , which was increased to 0.87 V after the secondary growth process. The increase of the $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ promoted the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and FF enhancement of the devices, which is mainly due to the optimization of the energy level structure of the perovskite film after secondary growth. Transient photovoltage (TPV) and photocurrent (TPC) measurements were used to analyze the carrier transport and recombination processes.[56] Figure 6h shows that the carrier lifetime of the target device is longer than that of the control device (5.0 vs $2.2\upmu\mathrm{s})$ ). The charge extraction time of the target device is shorter than that of the control device (1.27 vs $1.82~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ ) (Figure S22). This indicates that the carrier transport rate of the device was accelerated, and the nonradiative recombination process was significantly suppressed after the secondary growth approach. In order to further analyze the charge recombination behaviors in CPSCs, we conducted light intensity-dependent $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ tests. Figure 6i shows the relationship between $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and light intensity. The slope of the curve reduced from $2.00\ k_{\mathrm{B}}\mathrm{T}\ q^{-1}$ for the control device to $1.66k_{\mathrm{B}}\mathrm{T}q^{-1}$ for the target device $\lfloor k_{\mathrm{B}}$ is the Boltzmann constant, $T$ is the temperature, and $q$ is the basic charge). This suggests that the secondary growth strategy can effectively suppress the trapinduced recombination process in the corresponding devices.[57]
## 3. Conclusion
This work proposed a moisture-induced secondary crystal growth strategy to improve the crystalline quality of thick perovskite films. It was demonstrated that the moisture in the air interacted with the surface of perovskite grains and formed metastable hydrated intermediates, rendering the surface of perovskite grains reactive. These metastable intermediates were effectively used to trigger the secondary crystal growth at a certain temperature. This secondary growth strategy not only improved the crystallinity and morphology of the perovskite films, but also optimized energy level alignment by modulating the surface compositions of the film. As a result, the defect state density of the perovskite film was reduced, and the interface contact between the perovskite film and carbon electrode was improved. The efficiency of HTL-free C-PSCs was boosted to $19.52\%$ . This work not only promotes the development of C-PSCs, but also presents new insights into the effect of moisture on the structural properties of perovskite films.
